====Determiner====
{{head|en|determiner|cat2=possessive determiners}}

# [[my|My]]; belonging to [[me]].
## {{lb|en|archaic}} {{non-gloss definition|Used attributively after the noun it modifies.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|V|1|text={{...}} Flesh and blood, / You, brother '''mine''', that entertain'd ambition, / {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} {{non-gloss definition|Used attributively before a vowel.}}
##* '''1862''' February, [[w:Julia Ward Howe|Julia Ward Howe]], "{{w|The Battle Hymn of the Republic}}", in ''The Atlantic Monthly'', Volume IX, Number LII, page 10,
##*: '''Mine''' eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord: / {{...}}
##* '''1930''' Winter, Packard Motor Car Company, ''The Packard Magazine'', Volume 9, Number 2, page 6,
##*: '''Mine''' host, it seemed, did favors for everybody...

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|en|my|My}} and {{m|en||mine}} are essentially two forms of the same word, with {{m|en||my}} being used [[attributive]]ly before the noun, and {{m|en||mine}} being used in all other cases, as may be seen in most of the usage examples and quotations above. In this respect, this word is analogous to most of the other possessive pronouns (e.g. {{m|en|your}} vs. {{m|en|yours}}), as well as a number of other noun modifiers, such as {{m|en|lone}}/{{m|en|alone}}.
* Historically, {{m|en||my}} came to be used only before a consonant sound, and later came to be used regardless of the following sound. Nonetheless, {{m|en||mine}} still sees archaic pre-vocalic use, as may be seen in the 1862 quotation above, and in the most formal of writing even into the 20th century.

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|mine}}, from {{der|en|LL.|mina}}, from {{der|en|cel-gau|-}} (compare to {{cog|cy|mwyn}}, {{cog|ga|mianach||ore}}), from {{der|en|cel-pro|*mēnis||ore, metal}}.

